(* Blorp 2: local selective function imports, scalar bindings, fixed unions and
   records, tail matches, ordinary type selections and an explicit target library.

   This is a byte-level source grammar. No whitespace is implicit.
   Dialect: `=` defines a rule; `;` ends it; juxtaposition concatenates;
   `|` means alternatives; (...) groups; [...] is zero or one occurrence;
   {...} is zero or more occurrences; quoted strings are literal bytes;
   "A".."Z" is an inclusive single-byte range; comments use parentheses
   around asterisks. X - Y excludes the complete strings of Y from X.
   Grammar literal escapes are \n = LF (0x0A), \r = CR (0x0D), \t = TAB (0x09),
   \" = double quote (0x22), and \xNN = the byte with hexadecimal value NN.
   These grammar-literal escapes do not introduce source escapes.
   EOF succeeds only at the exact end of the source, consuming no byte.

   Lexical contracts:
   - Identifiers use the longest consecutive identifier byte run.
   - Every word GRAMMAR 1.4 reserves is reserved here: `func`, `pure`, `match`,
     `var`, `void`, `import`, `type`, `private`, `record`, `union`, `builtin`,
     `as`, `opaque`, `into_opaque`, `from_opaque`, `if`, `else`, `and`, `or`,
     `not`, `while`, `for`, `in`, `break`, `continue`, `alias`, `debug`,
     `detach`, `foreign`, `from`, `implements`, `resource`, `select`, `trait`,
     `try`, `where` and `_`. They are symbol
     tokens and never names; written where a name is declared they report
     "`word` is a reserved keyword and cannot be used as a name". The words
     `fixed`, `methods_only`, `tail_recursive` and the base type names are
     names in the host too: the syntax recognises them by identity where a
     declaration or annotation begins, and they remain valid names elsewhere.
     Lone `_` is a wildcard token; `_name` remains an identifier.
   - Integer tokens use the longest consecutive digit run, with an optional
     adjacent minus. Parsing rejects leading zeros and values outside
     -9223372036854775808..9223372036854775807. `-0` has value zero.
   - Source Float uses decimal digits with a fraction or exponent, no leading
     zeros, separators, hexadecimal forms or suffixes. Fractions need digits on
     both sides of the dot; exponent digits follow optional +/- immediately.
     Source overflow is rejected; subnormals and underflow to signed zero are
     accepted. Plus signs, separated minus signs and arithmetic operators are
     unsupported. A dot followed by a digit belongs to a Float token; other dots
     retain UFCS/field syntax, including `42.identity()` and `1.5.identity()`.
     A dot directly after an integer must be followed by fraction digits or,
     after optional spaces, a field or function name.
   - Arrow bytes stay adjacent, as do the two dots of `..`; `0..n` is an
     integer, the range symbol and a name. Spaces occur only where these rules permit.
   - Identifiers and comments remain ASCII; quoted values admit valid UTF-8.
     CR is unsupported as a raw byte, including in comments and quotes.
   - A `|` that is the first byte after a line's indentation, including a
     continuation line inside brackets, starts a pipe String: the text after
     each marker, verbatim, joined with LF, with no escapes. The literal
     continues on the next line when that line starts with the first marker
     line's exact indentation bytes and then `|`. The text starts after the
     single marker, so `||` yields one `|`; `${` is rejected. The word `raw`
     alone on a code line, followed by a marker line, starts a raw block: the
     same, but `${` is text. The last line's LF is the line's NEWLINE. Pipe
     text admits tabs, printable bytes and UTF-8.
   - A Char literal is one character or one escape pair between single
     quotes on one line. The lexer produces the token; the parser rejects it
     until the Char type exists.
   - Quoted String values occupy one physical line. Nine fixed escapes and
     \u{H} with one to six hexadecimal digits are accepted. Unicode escapes
     must denote scalars through 10FFFF, excluding D800..DFFF. Raw UTF-8 must
     use shortest scalar encodings. No normalization occurs. Length and spans
     count bytes. Raw ASCII controls and DEL are unsupported inside quotes.
   - `--` starts a single-line comment ending before LF or EOF. It takes
     precedence over a signed integer; `->` still forms an arrow.
   - A line holding only `---` at column 1, with optional trailing spaces,
     opens a documentation block that the next such line closes. The block
     is trivia; its text admits any byte but CR. `----` and `--- x` are
     comments. An unterminated block is an error at its opening fence.
   - Blank, whitespace-only and comment-only lines are lexical trivia. Their
     indentation has no layout meaning. Only lines containing code produce
     layout tokens, retaining their original byte offsets (see Layout below).

   Syntax accepts any unreserved function, parameter and type identifier.
   `fixed` is a contextual word in declaration headers and remains a valid
   function, type, parameter and variant identifier elsewhere; the reserved
   words listed under Lexical contract are never identifiers, and using one
   where a name is declared reports the keyword by spelling.
   Import blocks precede the module's declarations and select functions by their declared names from explicit ./ or leading
   ../ paths, or bare paths under an explicitly supplied target-library root.
   Paths omit .brp and contain adjacent identifier components; interior
   dot segments, wildcards and reexports are unsupported. `path as Alias`
   names the module; the alias may stand alone or precede ':' and
   selections. Symbol aliases are unsupported. A row names each selection, and
   each listed constructor, once; repetition is rejected during parsing. Explicit
   Union(First, Second) imports select a union and named constructors; Union()
   selects its type alone. Bare names resolve functions or types the target declares, without
   capitalization rules; a module never reexports what it imports.
   A type selection supplies target-local receiver functions for UFCS. The contextual
   suffix methods_only supplies receiver functions without importing the type or constructors.
   A declaration may start with `private`; private declarations remain local.
   Selections and qualified names expose only a target's own public declarations.
   The prelude is the only implicit import, and only
   user modules receive it; modules under the library root see only their own
   declarations and written imports, and cannot import user modules. The base
   module `_base` is imported like any module; the prelude cannot be imported
   explicitly.
   Every declaration has a program-wide identity and a defining module. Imports
   preserve that identity. Bodies and annotations resolve in their defining
   module. Import dependency cycles, including a module importing itself, are
   rejected at the import that closes them. Each source is loaded once.
   The checker requires exactly one nongeneric root-local `main` returning Int or Void,
   with no parameters or one List[String] parameter. Index 0 is the executable name.
   An omitted return annotation means Void. A pure function cannot return Void,
   including a generic instance whose concrete return type is Void.
   Int main returns its result as the process exit status; Void main exits 0.
   Helper parameter and return types are `Int`, `Float`, `String`, `Void`, List[E] for any supported finite E, a declared fixed union,
   a declared fixed record, or one of the function's declared rigid type parameters. Type parameters start
   with a capital ASCII letter and contain only ASCII letters and digits.
   Their names cannot collide with builtin or declared union/record type names.
   Every body is checked once, including unused generic declarations. Generic
   calls infer one consistent substitution from all arguments, then their known
   expected result. Nested calls share private expression-local constraints;
   caller-owned parameters remain rigid. A known nominal union or record head
   supplies constructor/literal context even when its arguments remain unsolved.
   A bare parameter supplies no nominal owner, including when another argument
   constrains it. A uniquely resolved UFCS contract supplies receiver context;
   overloads remain receiver-directed and are not ranked by expected returns.
   New bindings and match scrutinees synthesize without enclosing return context;
   discarded Void tails impose no result constraint. Zero-argument calls may
   infer parameters represented in an expected result. Remaining unconstrained
   or phantom parameters diagnose ambiguity, with no default type. Only complete
   substitutions and accepted facts are published. Root-local main cannot be generic. List repeat, length, get, append, set
   and concat builtin wrappers may be generic; other runtime builtin declarations cannot. Explicit call type arguments are deferred.
   Fixed unions may declare ordered type parameters. Written applications take ordered
   recursive type arguments: Int, Float, String, Void, in-scope parameters, records, ordinary
   unions or further applications. Variants have zero or more ordered fields,
   annotated with ordinary supported types, including applied records/unions,
   Lists and their own parameters. Empty declaration parentheses are rejected;
   a nullary variant has no parentheses. Unit(Void) has one real field. Concrete
   Lists admit every supported finite element type, including Void, records, unions
   and nested Lists. Finite inline
   unions own every active managed child occurrence without an allocated union
   root. Child layouts precede parents. Recursive data declarations remain
   unsupported, including unused and mixed record/union cycles.
   Phantom String applications remain valid. Instance identity includes the
   declaration and complete ordered concrete arguments, independently of layout.
   Type names occupy a separate module-local namespace from function and variant names.
   Union type names are unique, and a variant name is unique in its module:
   functions and the variants of every union in a module share one value
   namespace. Bare variants resolve in the expected union type, determined by the
   receiving function parameter or declared return type. Parameters, locals and
   field captures cannot reuse a visible function, constructor or module alias,
   or an enclosing parameter, local or capture. Type-only names are separate.
   A UFCS target resolves in the containing module; its receiver
   uses the containing function's scope. Calls require matching arity and
   nominal types, and pure callers calling only pure callees. Self and mutual
   function recursion are accepted. Generic call substitutions must not contain
   a parameter-dependency cycle that nests a parameter inside an application;
   bare-parameter permutations and paths ending in concrete resets remain finite.
   Unannotated calls use the ordinary C stack, without a constant-stack guarantee.
   A single @tail_recursive before optional pure/func requires nongeneric direct
   self recursion, with every self call in a whole function or match-arm tail.
   Direct and final UFCS self calls become explicit loop backedges at -O0.
   Arguments, receivers, binding initializers, scrutinees, constructor/record
   children and earlier postfix operations are non-tail. The signature and
   every evaluated value must be unmanaged, including unused initializers.
   Phantom String type arguments without managed payload are allowed.
   Zero-argument annotated helpers and main are allowed; termination is not proved.
   Annotations without direct self calls, generic annotations, and annotations
   on builtin bodies are rejected. Mutual calls remain ordinary calls. Recursive
   data declarations remain unsupported; cyclic runtime value graphs are forbidden.
   Functions without pure are impure. Nullary variants are bare values. A
   variant with fields uses an application with exactly that many ordered
   arguments, evaluated once from left to right. Constructor
   application is pure and is not a function call; constructor UFCS is deferred.
   Bindings, reassignments and expression statements share one source order.
   A non-final bare expression must return Void; other values must be bound
   or returned. Void has exactly one value, `void`, and is unmanaged in every
   value position. A final expression remains mandatory, even for Void functions.
   A Void function evaluates and discards its final expression (of any type),
   including each selected match-arm result, and returns the sole Void value.
   Discarded expressions still undergo ordinary name, type and purity checking.
   Matches follow any statement prefix and occupy the function tail. Their scrutinee must synthesize
   a union type from a binding or function result; a constructor alone does not
   provide a receiving type. A whole-arm bare name must denote a visible
   constructor of that union; an unknown name is an error and only `_` is a
   catch-all. Constructors from another module must be imported with their union.
   Bind the scrutinee before matching when an arm needs its whole value.
   Constructor patterns contain
   one pattern per declared field; checking accepts a binder or wildcard there
   and rejects a nested pattern until the pattern-compilation milestone. Wildcards preserve positions;
   binders in one pattern must be distinct. Nullary patterns allow Empty or
   Empty(). Field binders and wildcards are irrefutable. Checking requires
   exhaustive, useful arms and type-checks every arm result.
   Bindings resolve initializer names before adding a new local. `var`
   creates a mutable local under the same no-shadowing rule. Ordinary `=`
   reassigns a visible mutable binding, rejects a visible immutable binding,
   or creates a new immutable local. Duplicate declarations within one block
   are rejected. New local types are inferred; reassignment retains the established
   type. Bare constructors need a receiving type. Every initializer is
   checked and evaluated, even when its binding is unused. An arm may contain
   an inline expression or a depth-three block of statements and a final value.
   Arm-local declarations do not enter sibling scopes. New declarations check
   their initializer before a name collision; existing assignment errors precede
   the RHS. Parameter types are resolved before body-scope name validation.
   Fixed records are nonempty, may declare ordered type parameters, and contain
   ordinary supported finite field types. Their type names share the builtin/union
   type namespace; fields are unique within each record. Literals require a known
   nominal record head, contain each field exactly once, and evaluate in written
   order. Type arguments may still be inferred; field spellings never choose an
   owner. Empty literals parse but fail checking for missing fields. Field reads
   have no parentheses; UFCS calls have parentheses. A field read supplies no
   literal-owner hint. Records whose fields are Int, Float or recursively eligible
   inline fixed records remain inline. Other supported records have managed ARC
   roots, including those with Void or unmanaged-union fields. Copies and whole-value reassignment preserve
   aliases; each managed stored occurrence owns its child independently.
   Updates preserve the base's nominal type, evaluate it once, evaluate nonempty
   replacements in written order, and construct fresh declaration-ordered storage.
   Field assignment, empty/recursive records and multiline braces remain unsupported.
   Compiler-known builtin type syntax is parsed in ordinary modules and checked
   only in the configured _base.brp foundation role. That module owns Int,
   Float, String, Void and unary List exactly once, plus ordinary Bool/Option/Result
   unions, which the foundation check designates by name: Bool with exactly
   True and False, Option[A] with Some(A) and None, Result[Value, Error] with
   Ok(Value) and Err(Error), in any order. It imports nothing and declares no functions. prelude.brp is an
   ordinary module whose imports select base types and operation methods; it
   cannot be imported explicitly. No
   runtime-name fallback or positional prelude input remains.
   Closed builtin bodies are allowed as the sole body of module functions,
   without statements or tail annotations. List repeat, length, get, append, set
   and concat wrappers may be generic; other builtin wrappers must be nongeneric. A closed builtin label
   establishes the operation identity and ordinary checking validates its contract.
   Ordinary quoted expressions construct fresh owned String values. Builtin
   declarations match their quoted raw label exactly, without escape decoding.
   In module bodies, builtin is reserved: a body line starting with builtin
   is the intrinsic form, and anything other than an opening parenthesis and
   a quoted label after it is a syntax error.
   The pure string.parse_int builtin returns the designated Option[Int]; checking
   accepts that exact result and specialization translates the designated
   constructors to concrete identities. string.parse_float returns Option[Float],
   string.byte_at returns Option[Int] for an unsigned byte at a byte index,
   string.from_codepoint returns owning Option[String] for a Unicode scalar,
   list.get Option[E], and int.equals and string.equals the designated Bool.
   system.get_current_directory is impure, has no parameters, and returns the
   designated Result[String, String]. Both constructors own a fresh String.
   Native pathname bytes are validated before publication; OS failures return Err.
   Byte access rejects negative and out-of-bounds Int64 indices. Scalar encoding
   rejects negative values, surrogates and values above U+10FFFF before narrowing;
   NUL and noncharacters are valid. Some owns its fresh String payload.
   string.parse_float's counted decimal input permits a leading sign and zeros, rejects nonfinite
   or overflowing values, and preserves subnormals and signed zero on underflow.
   Float arithmetic is binary64, with zero divisors returning positive zero.
   float.to_string produces round-trip decimal text using 6..17 significant digits
   and canonical nan/inf/-inf/-0 spellings. No locale or rounding mode is changed.
   list.repeat, list.length and list.get retain their resolved element type through
   specialization. repeat evaluates E and count once; nonpositive counts produce
   empty Lists. Each initialized slot owns one occurrence. get returns an owned E
   in its explicit present constructor, independently of the List's lifetime;
   zero, Void, empty Strings and nullary variants do not imply absence. Negative
   or out-of-range indices select the empty constructor. Only final root release
   destroys initialized slots. A list literal constructs one fresh root of its
   elements; append, set and concat return fresh Lists.
   Option remains an ordinary library union. Int conversion consumes counted ASCII
   bytes completely and rejects whitespace, NUL, suffixes and Int64 overflow.
   Ownership admits borrowed managed parameters, locally owned prefixes and fresh
   arm-local managed values in an ordinary terminating tail match. Each selected
   arm discharges prefix and local obligations after securing its result, dropping
   unused/intermediate owners or transferring its managed result. Field extraction
   borrows the exact selected immutable parent value and acquires escaping children
   independently. Repeated stored children retain separate obligations despite
   sharing a value identity. Annotated loops remain unmanaged. A bound or
   statement match is a join: each selected arm moves its value, with an owner
   made in that arm, into the join, and owners live before it stay live
   through every arm.
*)

letter           = "A".."Z" | "a".."z" ;
digit            = "0".."9" ;
identifier_start = letter | "_" ;
identifier_byte  = identifier_start | digit ;
identifier       = (identifier_start { identifier_byte })
                 - ("func" | "pure" | "match" | "var" | "void" | "_" | "import" | "type" | "private" | "record"
                    | "union" | "builtin" | "as" | "opaque" | "into_opaque" | "from_opaque" | "if" | "else"
                    | "and" | "or" | "not" | "while" | "for" | "in" | "break" | "continue") ;

space            = " " ;
(* A line break inside an open bracket is trivia: a bracket_break may stand
   wherever spaces may, but only while more "(", "[" or "{" than ")", "]" or
   "}" have been read on the lines so far, and no bracketed block (see
   Layout) is open at that depth. The lexer enforces that condition; a closer
   with nothing open leaves the depth at zero. A column-1 fence line inside an
   open bracket is an error, not a documentation block, and a "|" first on a
   continuation line starts a pipe_string as on any line. *)
bracket_trivia_line = { horizontal } [ line_comment ] ( "\n" | EOF ) ;
bracket_break    = [ line_comment ] "\n" { bracket_trivia_line } { horizontal } ;
spaces           = { space | bracket_break } ;
required_spaces  = space spaces ;
ascii_byte       = "\x00".."\x7F" ;
comment_byte     = ascii_byte - ("\n" | "\r") ;
line_comment     = "--" { comment_byte } ;
horizontal       = space | "\t" ;
doc_fence        = "---" { space } ( "\n" | EOF ) ;
doc_text_line    = { ascii_byte - ( "\n" | "\r" ) | utf8_scalar | "\x80".."\xFF" } "\n" ;
doc_block        = doc_fence { doc_text_line - doc_fence } doc_fence ;
trivia_line      = { horizontal } [ line_comment ] ( "\n" | EOF ) | doc_block ;
indentation      = { "\t" } | { "    " } ;

(* Layout. The lexer turns line structure into the tokens NEWLINE, INDENT and
   DEDENT, which the rules below use instead of LF bytes and indentation.
   - Trivia lines produce no tokens. Every other line is a code line: an
     indentation prefix, then code. The prefix's level is its tab count or its
     count of four-space groups; any other prefix is an error.
   - Every code line ends with NEWLINE: its trailing spaces and optional
     comment, then its LF, or EOF when the source ends without one.
   - A code line deeper than the innermost open level starts with INDENT and
     opens its level, whatever the step (host 2.3, L2 case 4). A shallower
     code line starts with one DEDENT for each level it closes and must land
     on an open level; otherwise it is "inconsistent indentation". EOF closes
     every open level.
   - INDENT and DEDENT span the line's indentation prefix; at EOF they and
     the final NEWLINE are empty spans at the end of the source.
   - A line break inside an open bracket produces none of these tokens: the
     code line continues on the next line, whose indentation is not checked
     (see bracket_break). Layout resumes at the first line end at depth zero.
   - Bracketed blocks (host GRAMMAR 2.2-2.3): a ":" that ends a line inside
     brackets opens a block at that bracket depth, unless one is already open
     at that depth or deeper. While it is open, line breaks at that depth are
     significant again and its lines are laid out like any block: the first
     is deeper (at any width) and starts with INDENT, and a line dedented to
     the block's base closes it with DEDENT. Line breaks inside further brackets
     nested within the block are trivia again. A closing bracket never closes
     a block: the DEDENT comes with the next line, or at the end of the input.
     A line at the block's bracket depth indented strictly between the block's
     base and its level also closes it, dedenting to the base (L2 case 3).
   Blocks nest by INDENT and DEDENT alone; no rule names a depth. *)
NEWLINE          = spaces [ line_comment ] ( "\n" | EOF ) ;
INDENT           = indentation ;
DEDENT           = indentation ;

(* A function has one header followed by zero or more ordered statement lines and a
   mandatory expression line or tail match. A union has one header and one or more
   variant lines. An inline comment does not continue an expression onto a later
   code line. Spaces in a call are explicit, including f ( ) and 1 . f ( ). UFCS
   supplies its receiver as the first argument, followed by the ordered explicit
   arguments in its parentheses. All expressions stay on one line, including
   scrutinees and arm results. A function's last match is its tail; an earlier
   match is a bound or statement match, and an arm may end with a nested
   match. Non-final expression statements
   must have Void type. Void has the sole value void. A Void function evaluates
   and discards its final expression before returning void. *)
nonzero_digit    = "1".."9" ;
integer          = [ "-" ] ("0" | nonzero_digit { digit }) ;
exponent         = ("e" | "E") [ "+" | "-" ] digit { digit } ;
float            = integer (("." digit { digit } [ exponent ]) | exponent) ;
hex_digit        = digit | "A".."F" | "a".."f" ;
unicode_escape   = "u{" hex_digit [ hex_digit [ hex_digit [ hex_digit [ hex_digit [ hex_digit ] ] ] ] ] "}" ;
string_escape    = "\\" ("n" | "r" | "t" | "0" | "\\" | "\"" | "'" | "{" | "}" | unicode_escape) ;
utf8_tail        = "\x80".."\xBF" ;
utf8_scalar      = "\xC2".."\xDF" utf8_tail
                 | "\xE0" "\xA0".."\xBF" utf8_tail
                 | ("\xE1".."\xEC" | "\xEE".."\xEF") utf8_tail utf8_tail
                 | "\xED" "\x80".."\x9F" utf8_tail
                 | "\xF0" "\x90".."\xBF" utf8_tail utf8_tail
                 | "\xF1".."\xF3" utf8_tail utf8_tail utf8_tail
                 | "\xF4" "\x80".."\x8F" utf8_tail utf8_tail ;
string_byte      = "\x20".."\x21" | "\x23".."\x5B" | "\x5D".."\x7E" | utf8_scalar ;
string_contents  = { string_byte | string_escape } ;
(* Raw interpolation openers are excluded; $\{ remains literal text. *)
string_literal   = "\"" (string_contents - (string_contents "${" string_contents)) "\"" ;
char_byte        = "\x20".."\x26" | "\x28".."\x5B" | "\x5D".."\x7E" | utf8_scalar ;
char_literal     = "'" ( char_byte | "\\" ( ascii_byte - "\n" ) ) "'" ;
pipe_byte        = "\t" | "\x20".."\x7E" | utf8_scalar ;
pipe_text        = { pipe_byte } - ( { pipe_byte } "${" { pipe_byte } ) ;
raw_text         = { pipe_byte } ;
(* Every marker after the first follows the first marker line's exact
   indentation bytes; the lexer compares them. A pipe_string begins only
   where a line's code begins, so a "|" after other code on its line is the
   record update bar. *)
pipe_string      = "|" pipe_text { "\n" indentation "|" pipe_text } ;
raw_string       = "raw" { space } "\n" indentation "|" raw_text { "\n" indentation "|" raw_text } ;
(* Every bracketed, comma-separated list admits one trailing comma. *)
trailing_comma   = [ spaces "," ] ;
arguments        = expression { spaces "," spaces expression } trailing_comma ;
direct_call      = identifier spaces "(" spaces [ arguments ] spaces ")" ;
record_field     = identifier spaces "=" spaces expression ;
record_literal   = "{" spaces [ record_field { spaces "," spaces record_field } trailing_comma ] spaces "}" ;
record_update    = "{" spaces expression spaces "|" spaces record_field { spaces "," spaces record_field } trailing_comma spaces "}" ;
(* A group is transparent: its expression is the result, and postfix
   operations may follow the closing parenthesis. *)
group            = "(" spaces expression spaces ")" ;
(* A qualified form is minted only when its first identifier is a module
   alias of the import block, even when a local of that spelling is in
   scope; otherwise `name . name` is a UFCS call or field read. Checking
   rejects qualified forms until the namespace milestone. *)
(* The conversion words are reserved: `into_opaque` and `from_opaque` always
   start the form and require the opaque type name and a parenthesised value. *)
opaque_conversion = ( "into_opaque" | "from_opaque" ) required_spaces identifier spaces "(" spaces expression spaces ")" ;
qualified_name   = identifier spaces "." spaces identifier ;
qualified_call   = qualified_name spaces "(" spaces [ arguments ] spaces ")" ;
(* Every element has one type: the expected List's element when the context
   supplies one, otherwise the first element's; an empty literal needs that
   context. *)
list_literal     = "[" spaces [ arguments ] spaces "]" ;
primary          = "void" | float | integer | string_literal | pipe_string | raw_string | qualified_call | qualified_name
                 | opaque_conversion | identifier | direct_call | list_literal | record_literal | record_update | group ;
receiver_call    = spaces "." spaces identifier spaces "(" spaces [ arguments ] spaces ")" ;
field_read       = spaces "." spaces identifier ;
(* Precedence, lowest first, as in the host: "or", "and", "not", "..",
   then postfix operations; "or" and "and" associate left. A range has no
   postfix operations of its own, and "not" cannot be a range end without
   parentheses. and, or, not and ranges parse; checking rejects them until
   the join milestone (ranges until the loops milestone), as does an
   if-expression. A line break after
   `and` or `or` is accepted only inside brackets; the host also accepts it
   bare (GRAMMAR 3.5), which is a follow-up. *)
(* A control form ends its expression at its last block's DEDENT: no
   postfix operation, operator or range continues it on the next line, and
   the line it ends needs no NEWLINE of its own. `else if` nests the next
   if-expression as the else branch. The lexer's longest-identifier rule
   makes `iftrue` a name, so `if` is followed by a space or a bracket. A
   match is a control form everywhere but at a function's tail, where it is
   the body itself (`tail`). *)
control          = if_expr | match_body | lambda ;
(* A lambda body starts a new statement context (host 5.3): loop controls
   need a loop of its own, and `?=` is invalid anywhere inside it (owner
   rule; the host accepts it). Inside brackets a
   block body is a bracketed block, which must end before the bracket closes
   (host 4.9): a closing bracket on the block's last line is an error. *)
lambda           = [ "pure" required_spaces ] "func" spaces "(" spaces [ lambda_param { spaces "," spaces lambda_param } trailing_comma ] spaces ")"
                   [ spaces "->" spaces type_annotation ] spaces ":" ( spaces expression | NEWLINE INDENT block DEDENT ) ;
lambda_param     = ( identifier | "_" ) [ spaces ":" spaces type_annotation ] ;
if_expr          = "if" spaces expression spaces ":" NEWLINE INDENT block DEDENT [ else_branch ] ;
else_branch      = "else" spaces ":" NEWLINE INDENT block DEDENT | "else" required_spaces if_expr ;
(* Method chains continue across lines (host 4.2): a line starting with "."
   at the same indentation, or one level deeper (an indented chain, whose
   INDENT the chain owns and whose DEDENT ends it), continues the postfix
   operations. The same-indentation leading dot follows the host's current
   behaviour while GRAMMAR D4 is open. Inside an indented chain every line
   starts with ".". A chain that ends inline, with an operator after its
   last line, leaves its owned DEDENTs to the enclosing statement, which
   consumes them after its line end (host D4); an `if`, `while`, `for` or
   `match` header has no line end to consume them, so an indented chain
   there is an error. *)
indented_chain   = NEWLINE INDENT "." ( receiver_call | field_read ) { receiver_call | field_read | NEWLINE "." ( receiver_call | field_read ) | indented_chain } [ NEWLINE ] DEDENT ;
operand          = control | primary { receiver_call | field_read | NEWLINE "." ( receiver_call | field_read ) | indented_chain } ;
range_expr       = operand [ spaces ".." spaces operand ] ;
not_expr         = "not" required_spaces not_expr | range_expr ;
and_expr         = not_expr { required_spaces "and" required_spaces not_expr } ;
or_expr          = and_expr { required_spaces "or" required_spaces and_expr } ;
expression       = or_expr ;
(* In a type, a dot can only qualify, so no alias set is consulted. *)
type_arguments   = "[" spaces type_annotation { spaces "," spaces type_annotation } trailing_comma spaces "]" ;
type_annotation  = identifier [ spaces "." spaces identifier ] spaces [ type_arguments ] ;
parameter        = identifier spaces ":" spaces type_annotation ;
type_parameter   = "A".."Z" { letter | digit } ;
type_parameters = "[" spaces type_parameter { spaces "," spaces type_parameter } trailing_comma spaces "]" spaces ;
function_header  = [ "pure" required_spaces ] "func" required_spaces identifier spaces [ type_parameters ] "(" spaces
                   [ parameter { spaces "," spaces parameter } trailing_comma ] spaces ")" spaces [ "->" spaces type_annotation spaces ] ":"
                   NEWLINE ;
tail_annotation  = "@tail_recursive" (required_spaces | NEWLINE) ;
function         = [ tail_annotation ] function_header INDENT body DEDENT ;
(* A constructor's fields are patterns, so patterns nest. Checking accepts
   one binder or wildcard per field and rejects a deeper field until the
   pattern-compilation milestone. *)
(* Alternatives separated by "|" form an or-pattern, rejected by checking
   until the pattern-compilation milestone. *)
(* A list pattern takes no trailing comma, as in the host; the rest comes
   last. Checking rejects list patterns until the pattern-compilation
   milestone. *)
spread_target    = "..." spaces ( identifier | "_" ) ;
list_pattern     = "[" spaces ( [ spread_target ] | pattern { spaces "," spaces pattern } [ spaces "," spaces spread_target ] ) spaces "]" ;
(* String and Char literal patterns are rejected by checking until the
   pattern-compilation milestone. *)
(* A dot in a pattern can only qualify a constructor by a module alias;
   checking rejects qualified heads until the namespace milestone. *)
pattern_head     = identifier [ spaces "." spaces identifier ] ;
alternative      = "_" | string_literal | char_literal | list_pattern
                 | pattern_head spaces [ "(" spaces [ pattern { spaces "," spaces pattern } trailing_comma ] spaces ")" ] ;
pattern          = alternative { spaces "|" spaces alternative } ;
(* After '=' the value is on the same line, or alone on an indented next
   line whose DEDENT closes it. *)
initializer      = spaces expression NEWLINE | NEWLINE INDENT expression NEWLINE DEDENT ;
(* A binding may annotate its type; checking rejects the annotation until
   the join milestone. *)
binding          = [ "var" required_spaces ] identifier [ spaces ":" spaces type_annotation ] spaces "=" initializer ;
(* `name ?= value` binds the success payload or returns the failure; it is
   never `var` and is valid only among a function body's own top-level
   statements, never inside an if/match block, loop body or lambda body,
   including blocks within while conditions and for iterables (owner rule;
   the host keeps its existing rules). Checking rejects even valid top-level
   propagation until the join milestone. *)
propagation      = identifier [ spaces ":" spaces type_annotation ] spaces "?=" initializer ;
(* Loops are statements with no value. `break` and `continue` are valid only
   inside a loop body (host 5.3); neither may sit in an `if` or `match` used as
   a value within a loop (owner rule). A function body starts a new context.
   The parser enforces these; checking rejects loops until the loops milestone. *)
while_loop       = "while" spaces expression spaces ":" NEWLINE INDENT block DEDENT ;
for_loop         = "for" required_spaces ( identifier | "_" ) required_spaces "in" required_spaces expression spaces ":" NEWLINE INDENT block DEDENT ;
statement        = binding | while_loop | for_loop | "break" NEWLINE | "continue" NEWLINE | expression NEWLINE ;
function_statement = propagation | statement ;
(* A block's value is its last item when that item is an expression; a block
   may end after a statement and then has none. Where an expression is a
   control form, its final DEDENT stands in for the NEWLINE that ends the
   statement, initializer or value. Whether a block without a
   value is allowed where it stands is the checker's decision: a tail match's
   arm must have one. *)
block            = { statement } [ expression NEWLINE ] ;
(* A match case is the one place a statement may share the line with its
   header (host case_body); such an arm has no value. *)
match_arm        = pattern spaces ":" (spaces statement | spaces expression NEWLINE | NEWLINE INDENT block DEDENT) ;
match_body       = "match" required_spaces expression spaces ":" NEWLINE INDENT match_arm { match_arm } DEDENT ;
(* A function's last match is its tail body; a match followed by further
   lines is an expression statement. *)
tail             = expression NEWLINE | match_body ;
body             = { function_statement } tail | builtin_body ;
variant          = identifier spaces [ "(" spaces type_annotation { spaces "," spaces type_annotation } trailing_comma spaces ")" ] NEWLINE ;
fixed_union      = "fixed" required_spaces "union" required_spaces identifier spaces [ type_parameters ] ":"
                   NEWLINE INDENT variant { variant } DEDENT ;
record_member    = identifier spaces ":" spaces type_annotation ;
fixed_record     = "fixed" required_spaces "record" required_spaces identifier spaces [ type_parameters ] "{" spaces
                   record_member { spaces "," spaces record_member } trailing_comma spaces "}" NEWLINE ;
(* Opaque types and their conversions are read ahead of their semantics;
   checking rejects them until the recursive-types milestone. *)
opaque_type      = "opaque" required_spaces "type" required_spaces identifier spaces "=" spaces type_annotation NEWLINE ;
(* Module constants require annotated Int, Float or String literal initializers.
   Record, constant-reference, opaque-conversion and call initializers remain
   rejected in this increment; ordinary reads may be imported or qualified. *)
constant         = identifier spaces ":" spaces type_annotation spaces "=" initializer ;
declaration      = [ "private" required_spaces ] ( function | fixed_union | fixed_record | opaque_type | constant ) | builtin_type ;
relative_prefix  = "./" | "../" { "../" } ;
module_components = identifier { "/" identifier } ;
module_path      = [ relative_prefix ] module_components ;
constructor_selection = spaces "(" spaces [ identifier { spaces "," spaces identifier } trailing_comma ] spaces ")" ;
import_symbol    = identifier [ constructor_selection | required_spaces "methods_only" ] ;
import_selection = import_symbol { spaces "," spaces import_symbol } trailing_comma ;
(* The selections follow the path on its line, or on an indented block of
   lines, each holding one or more selections. *)
selection_line   = import_selection NEWLINE ;
module_alias     = required_spaces "as" required_spaces identifier ;
import_row       = module_path ( [ module_alias ] spaces ":" ( spaces import_selection NEWLINE
                   | NEWLINE INDENT selection_line { selection_line } DEDENT )
                   | module_alias NEWLINE ) ;
import_block     = "import" spaces ":" NEWLINE INDENT import_row { import_row } DEDENT ;
program          = { import_block } { declaration } EOF ;

(* Foundation declarations use the ordinary module parser.
   An empty module parses; designated foundation validation requires five builtins
   and the three designated unions. *)
builtin_type     = "type" required_spaces (("Int" | "Float" | "String" | "Void") spaces
                   | "List" spaces "[" spaces identifier spaces "]" spaces) "=" spaces "builtin" NEWLINE ;
builtin_label    = "\"int.to_string\"" | "\"string.length\"" | "\"string.print\""
                   | "\"int.add\"" | "\"int.subtract\"" | "\"int.multiply\""
                   | "\"int.divide\"" | "\"int.remainder\""
                   | "\"float.add\"" | "\"float.subtract\"" | "\"float.multiply\""
                   | "\"float.divide\"" | "\"float.remainder\"" | "\"float.to_string\""
                   | "\"list.repeat\"" | "\"list.length\""
                   | "\"list.append\"" | "\"list.set\"" | "\"list.concat\""
                   | "\"string.parse_int\"" | "\"string.parse_float\"" | "\"list.get\""
                   | "\"string.byte_at\"" | "\"string.from_codepoint\""
                   | "\"system.get_current_directory\""
                   | "\"int.equals\"" | "\"string.equals\"" ;
builtin_body     = "builtin" spaces "(" spaces builtin_label spaces ")" NEWLINE ;
